<p>Harker Heights is in western Bell County on the US-190/I-14 corridor, bordering Killeen and a short drive from Fort Cavazos (formerly Fort Hood). Stillhouse Hollow Lake lies just to the south. The city has hillier ground than Temple and a lot of residential growth, and many households have a connection to the military. From our base in Temple, we drive west along US-190 to reach Harker Heights.</p>

<h2>Homes and Roofs Across Harker Heights</h2>
<h3>Hillside and view lots</h3>
<p>Parts of Harker Heights sit on rolling hills, and homes on high ground or open slopes take more direct wind than those in sheltered spots. On these roofs we often find shingles lifted along the windward edges, loose ridge caps and wind-driven rain getting in at vents and roof-to-wall joints.</p>

<h3>Newer subdivisions</h3>
<p>Much of the city's housing was built in the last few decades, often with multi-level roof lines, many valleys and large roof areas. Those designs look great but create more places for water to collect and more flashing to get right. If you are in an HOA, check with your HOA about shingle color and style before replacing your roof.</p>

<h3>Rental homes and military families</h3>
<p>With Fort Cavazos close by, Harker Heights has many rental homes and owners who may be stationed elsewhere. Military moves can leave a house in the care of tenants or a property manager for years. We work with owners from a distance, coordinate access with tenants, and send photos and written estimates so decisions can be made remotely. If you are preparing to sell or rent out a home before a move, an inspection can flag roof issues early.</p>

<h2>Weather Exposure in Western Bell County</h2>
<p>Harker Heights sees the same Central Texas storm cycle as Temple, with spring as the peak season for large hail and damaging straight-line winds and a secondary season in the fall. Hilly terrain affects where wind hits hardest, and hailstorms can be highly localized, damaging one neighborhood heavily while sparing the next. Summer heat and strong UV break down shingles, sealants and pipe boots year after year.</p>

